# {{project_name}}

A merkle airdrop: eligible addresses claim once against an on-chain merkle
root, generated by
[soroban-forge](https://github.com/soroban-forge-labs/soroban-forge) from the
`merkle-airdrop` template.

## How it works

The allowlist can be arbitrarily large without paying to store it on-chain —
only its 32-byte root lives in the contract:

1. **Off-chain** — build a merkle tree over the `(address, amount)` entries.
2. **`initialize`** — the admin stores the root and funds the contract.
3. **`claim(claimant, amount, proof)`** — the contract recomputes the leaf,
   walks the proof up to the root and, on a match, marks the claimant and
   transfers the tokens.

Hashing rules an off-chain builder has to reproduce:

- **leaf** — `sha256(xdr(address) || be_bytes(amount))`. The amount is part of
  the leaf, so a claimant cannot ask for more than their entry.
- **pair** — `sha256(min(a, b) || max(a, b))`. Sorting the pair makes the proof
  order-independent, so it carries only sibling hashes and no left/right flags.

The `leaf` entrypoint is public so a script can cross-check its own hashing
against the contract's before publishing a root.

## Interface

| function                            | description                                    |
|-------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------|
| `initialize(admin, token, root)`    | store the merkle root and the token            |
| `fund(amount)`                      | admin deposits the tokens to be claimed        |
| `claim(claimant, amount, proof)`    | claim once against the current root            |
| `verify(claimant, amount, proof)`   | read-only eligibility check for a UI           |
| `has_claimed(claimant)`             | whether that address already claimed           |
| `set_root(root)`                    | admin replaces the root (e.g. a second round)  |
| `sweep()`                           | admin reclaims what is left after the window   |

Claim markers are written to persistent storage and their TTL is extended as
they are created, so a claimant cannot get a second payout by waiting for the
entry to be archived.

## Develop

```sh
cargo test                 # run unit tests
stellar contract build     # build the deployable wasm
```

## Deploy to testnet

```sh
stellar contract deploy \
  --wasm target/wasm32v1-none/release/{{crate_name}}.wasm \
  --source-account <your-identity> \
  --network testnet
```

Publish the root and fund the airdrop:

```sh
stellar contract invoke \
  --id <CONTRACT_ID> \
  --source-account <ADMIN> \
  --network testnet \
  -- initialize \
  --admin <ADMIN_ADDRESS> \
  --token <TOKEN_ADDRESS> \
  --root <64_HEX_CHARS>

stellar contract invoke \
  --id <CONTRACT_ID> \
  --source-account <ADMIN> \
  --network testnet \
  -- fund --amount 1000000
```

An eligible address claims with its proof:

```sh
stellar contract invoke \
  --id <CONTRACT_ID> \
  --source-account <CLAIMANT> \
  --network testnet \
  -- claim \
  --claimant <CLAIMANT_ADDRESS> \
  --amount 100 \
  --proof '["<SIBLING_HASH_HEX>","<UNCLE_HASH_HEX>"]'
```

